    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "A cluster of fault-normal joints occurs in a region of several overlapping, parallel normal faults in the Rough and Rocky Mesa region of Arches National Park, Utah. The hypothesis being promoted here is that joints grew in a perturbed stress field produced around the normal faults during fault evolution. The joints are approximately vertical, although dips are less (circa 60°) at the top of the unit (Moab Member of the Entrada Formation) within which the joints are completely contained. Features such as tail cracks, horsetail fractures, and veers indicate that joints were subject to shear stresses at some stage after their development. One example will be described in which the resultant joint geometry is liable to misinterpretation without a close examination of field relationships, It is not intuitively obvious that joints should form at such high angles to the faults they are associated with, however, field observations of features such as cross- cutting relationships and the spatial distribution of joint densities indicate that jointing was associated with faulting. In addition, preliminary numerical modeling using a boundary element method based program and principles of linear elastic fracture mechanics,predicts joint grow that high angles to the faults in the vicinity of the fault tips.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
